      <h2>Dallas Tiny House Builders Who Know the Rules Change at Every City Line</h2>
      <p>The Dallas area is not one market — it is dozens of cities, each with its own zoning code, permit office and attitude toward small homes. A backyard cottage that sails through in one suburb may need a public hearing in the next, and a tiny home on wheels that is welcome in a Denton County RV park may not be allowed as a full-time residence on a city lot. As tiny house builders in Dallas, Cowboy Tiny Homes helps you figure out where your project fits before you spend money on land, drawings or a trailer.</p>
      <p>Once we know what is allowed, we design a home that suits North Texas: tough enough for spring hail and summer heat, set on a foundation that handles shifting clay, and laid out so every square foot earns its place.</p>

      <h2>Dallas ADU and Tiny Home Regulations</h2>
      <p>The City of Dallas has historically been one of the more restrictive large Texas cities for accessory dwelling units. In many single-family zoning districts, a detached ADU intended as a rental has required a special exception from the Board of Adjustment, which means an application, a hearing and conditions such as owner occupancy. The city has been reviewing and debating changes to its housing and ADU rules, so the requirements may have changed — confirm the current code with Dallas Development Services before you plan around it.</p>
      <p>Suburbs vary widely. Some allow guest quarters or ADUs by right with size and setback limits, others restrict them to large lots or prohibit separate kitchens. Outside city limits in Collin, Denton, Kaufman and Ellis counties you will mostly deal with deed restrictions, septic permits, floodplain rules and utility availability rather than zoning.</p>
      <div class="callout callout--warn"><p><strong>Don't buy the trailer or the lot first.</strong> Let Cowboy Tiny Homes run a feasibility study for your exact address. We check zoning, deed restrictions, easements and utilities so you know your options. More background is in our <a href="/guides/tiny-house-laws-texas/">Texas tiny house laws guide</a>.</p></div>

      <h2>Tiny Homes We Build Across Dallas–Fort Worth</h2>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong><a href="/custom-tiny-homes-texas/">Custom tiny and small homes</a></strong> on a permanent foundation, designed to IRC code for full-time living on infill and rural lots.</li>
        <li><strong><a href="/adu-builders-texas/">Backyard ADUs and guest quarters</a></strong> where local code allows — for parents, adult children, a studio or rental income.</li>
        <li><strong><a href="/tiny-homes-on-wheels-texas/">Tiny homes on wheels</a></strong> for RV parks, family land and owners who may move.</li>
        <li><strong><a href="/park-model-homes-texas/">Park model homes</a></strong> for lake property around Lake Lewisville, Lake Ray Hubbard and Lake Texoma, and for weekend land east of the metro.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Built for North Texas Weather and Soil</h2>
      <div class="table-wrap"><table>
        <thead><tr><th>DFW challenge</th><th>How we design for it</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Large hail and spring storms</td><td>Impact-rated roofing options, standing-seam metal roofs, durable fiber-cement siding</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Expansive Blackland clay</td><td>Engineered post-tension slabs or pier-and-beam foundations, positive drainage away from the home</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Summer heat over 100°F</td><td>Radiant-barrier decking, continuous insulation, shaded windows and right-sized heat pumps</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Winter freezes</td><td>Protected plumbing runs, insulated skirting on trailer homes and freeze-aware utility hookups</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tornado risk</td><td>Strong roof-to-wall connections and anchoring; storm shelter options on larger lots</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p>Homes on wheels need extra thought here. We recommend tie-downs and skirting for any THOW parked long term, and we design weight distribution so the home tows safely on area highways.</p>

      <h2>Why Dallas Homeowners Are Building Small</h2>
      <p>Rising home prices and property taxes have pushed many DFW families to look for more flexible options. A small, well-built home lets retirees downsize near their grandkids, gives young buyers a way into homeownership on cheaper land outside the loop, and gives multigenerational households privacy without a second mortgage. Investors and hosts look at tiny homes and ADUs as income-producing space where rules permit — model the numbers with our <a href="/tools/adu-rental-income-calculator/">ADU rental income calculator</a>.</p>

      <h2>Communities We Serve Around Dallas</h2>
      <p>Cowboy Tiny Homes works throughout <strong>Dallas County</strong> — including Garland, Mesquite, Irving, Richardson and Grand Prairie — plus Plano, McKinney, Frisco, Allen and Wylie in <strong>Collin County</strong>, and Denton, Lewisville, Little Elm and Flower Mound in <strong>Denton County</strong>. We also build on rural land in Kaufman, Rockwall, Ellis and Hunt counties. Across the metroplex, see our page for <a href="/tiny-home-builders-fort-worth/">tiny home builders in Fort Worth</a>.</p>

      <h2>What a Dallas Tiny House Costs</h2>
      <p>Your budget depends on home type, size, finishes, foundation and how far utilities need to run. As a rule of thumb, builder-built tiny homes on wheels often start in the mid-$40,000s, park models are similar, and permanent ADUs and small homes range from about $60,000 into the low $200,000s and beyond. Plug your own numbers into the <a href="/tools/tiny-home-cost-calculator/">tiny home cost calculator</a>, and read our full guide to the <a href="/guides/cost-to-build-a-tiny-house-in-texas/">cost to build a tiny house in Texas</a>.</p>
